WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.006 --> 00:00:02.519
[SOUND]

00:00:02.519 --> 00:00:05.920
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.758
I was about six months pregnant with my
daughter and a neighbor asked me,

00:00:10.758 --> 00:00:14.420
Julie, will you go back to work after the
baby is born?

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:17.210
All I could think was.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:18.780
Are you kidding me?

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.010
Of course I'm gonna go back to work after
the baby's born.

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:24.650
My identity was tied to my career's
success.

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:29.000
But it was only a few short months later.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:31.350
I was holding my daughter.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:35.290
And all I could think was are you kidding
me?

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.135
Of course I am not going back to work.

00:00:38.135 --> 00:00:43.520
[LAUGH]
How was I to reconcile these 2 identities?

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.320
How was I going to define my own
happiness?

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.810
I realized then that the page wrote in
front of me didn't lead to my happiness.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.380
I was going to have to blaze my own trail.

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.520
Now what do I mean by that?

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:01.660
I mean societal norms had left women
choosing between staying at home and

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:03.710
career oriented.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:06.540
But I guess I just didn't see the world as
so black and white.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.029
And I needed to figure out the way to my
happiness.

00:01:12.029 --> 00:01:15.441
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:19.480
So I've spent the last 13 years blazing my
own trail.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.990
I've cofounded a company and I've raised a
family.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:25.430
And the trail is far from complete.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.890
But I've learned a few things along the
way.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:33.230
I've learned that first and foremost, you
must define your own happiness.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:33.900
Ignore the critics.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.860
I've learned that creative solutions are
often times the key to success.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:42.490
And I've learned that it's worth it.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:46.820
It's worth the blood, sweat, and tears in
order to find your own happiness.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.860
So, how will you define your happiness?

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:54.190
Will you blaze your own trail?

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.450
I stand here before you, with the
experiences of a mom.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:07.070
But, the reality is that dads often times
have the same questions.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:10.790
Not to mention, the need to love and
support the mothers in their lives.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.650
Understanding what would make me happy,
was a journey.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:22.190
I spent long hours at a hedge fund and
tried to balance that with a family.

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:22.860
I was miserable.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:29.430
And when I tried to just stay at home, I
wasn't content either.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.420
I needed to find a way, to a, for a
creative solution.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.820
My husband and I cofounded a company.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.870
And the naysayers said we were crazy to
start a family with a young,

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.660
to start a company with a young family.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:48.140
But they didn't share my priorities.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:53.260
As my own boss I had the leverage to
create value and

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:55.720
a license to pursue my ideas.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:00.390
But at the same time I had flexibility to
work half days and

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:02.520
be at home with my daughter.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:03.520
We kept strict rules.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.420
Between 5 and 8 p.m.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.020
was sacred.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.740
We had dinner together and we traveled
very little.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:14.300
And, yes, there were days when we had to
tell clients that we simply couldn't make

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:14.910
it to a meeting.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.830
One crucial and important meeting,

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:22.220
we missed in order to be at our son's
kindergarten graduation.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:29.100
As I stand here now and look back, with
hind sight being twenty twenty.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.270
My choices seem obvious to me.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.900
Let me be clear, it wasn't easy.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.460
We sacrificed company growth for our
family priorities.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:43.930
And there are so many times where I felt
like I sucked both at being a mom and

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:45.905
a manager, or today a student.

00:03:45.905 --> 00:03:49.800
But we stayed committed to our path.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:57.078
Now, there's a term for this lifestyle
choice, the Mompreneur.

00:03:57.078 --> 00:04:00.900
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Lisa Stone rocked this model.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.720
After the birth of her son,

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:09.150
much to everyone's surprise, she left her
job in the newsroom at CNN.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.430
In order to blaze her own path.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:12.525
Her creative solution?

00:04:12.525 --> 00:04:15.600
BlogHer.com.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.490
Now again, the naysayers said, she's
crazy, women aren't going to go online.

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:26.540
But she leveraged the fact that she was a
single mom to a one and a half year old,

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:31.130
in Silicon Valley, to know that this
really was a possibility.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:38.310
But this talk is not about being an
entrepreneur,

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.660
working full time, working part time, or
staying at home.

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:46.590
It's about knowing if you keep an openness
to creative solutions,

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:48.797
the possibilities are endless.

00:04:48.797 --> 00:04:51.375
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:04:51.375 --> 00:04:54.508
So there was another pivotal moment in my
life.

00:04:54.508 --> 00:04:59.490
[LAUGH]
I had three school aged children.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.070
I was on the verge of selling my company.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:05.548
And I'd been accepted to the GSB.

00:05:05.548 --> 00:05:11.610
On many accounts, I had accomplished what
I set out to do.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:19.075
It was the day before April orientation,
and I found out I was pregnant.

00:05:19.075 --> 00:05:23.545
[LAUGH]
And holy crap, twins.

00:05:23.545 --> 00:05:29.983
[LAUGH] As I began to look up at my new
reality.

00:05:29.983 --> 00:05:31.655
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:05:31.655 --> 00:05:35.694
I was prepared to set aside my life long
dream of attending Stanford.

00:05:35.694 --> 00:05:37.134
I was a mom.

00:05:37.134 --> 00:05:39.666
I knew what I had to do and I was prepared
to do it.

00:05:39.666 --> 00:05:41.336
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:05:41.336 --> 00:05:43.893
When we sat down to tell our other

00:05:43.893 --> 00:05:47.792
children about the twins entrance to our
lives.

00:05:47.792 --> 00:05:53.168
My daughter, about eleven years old,
looked up at me and

00:05:53.168 --> 00:05:58.659
said, Mom, you're still gonna go to
Stanford, right?

00:05:58.659 --> 00:05:59.610
You have to.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.676
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:06:02.676 --> 00:06:07.570
So I stand here now, blazing through my
own doubts.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:10.895
Blazing a path.

00:06:10.895 --> 00:06:13.435
As the mom with the most number of
children at the GSB.

00:06:13.435 --> 00:06:17.064
[LAUGH]

00:06:17.064 --> 00:06:21.509
Perhaps ever.

00:06:21.509 --> 00:06:27.900
But, but this was my dream and I knew I
had to do it, when my daughter asked me,

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:32.170
I knew I would have to figure out a way to
come and I'm here.

00:06:34.868 --> 00:06:38.250
Theresa Taylor in her book The Balanced
Myth

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:43.000
says blending your life together is really
about being authentic all the time.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:48.530
I don't think of myself as someone with
two different lives or

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:50.110
two different places to be.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:51.890
This is me.

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:53.930
This is who I am.

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:57.060
I love this notion.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.580
I subscribe to it completely.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:03.180
My li, my family is a part of everything I
do.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.100
They're a part of my authentic self.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:11.370
So when I started writing this talk a few
months ago.

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:13.010
Stanford assigned me a speech coach.

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:17.800
I knew even then that I wanted to include
my family as part of the talk.

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:25.645
I excitedly told him my plan, but he was
less than enthusiastic.

00:07:25.645 --> 00:07:28.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:30.480
&gt;&gt; But, Julie, so much could go wrong.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:31.900
And he's right.

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:35.160
My twins might get up here and start
crying.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.330
My teenager daughter, she's unlikely to
smile.

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:43.110
And my boys, well they could poke at each
other in front of all of you.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:48.474
But how was I to get up here and

00:07:48.474 --> 00:07:53.829
give a talk about integrating work and

00:07:53.829 --> 00:07:59.352
family while ignoring the naysayers,

00:07:59.352 --> 00:08:03.550
without doing exactly that?

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:05.943
So let me introduce to you.

00:08:05.943 --> 00:08:11.682
[APPLAUSE] [APPLAUSE] This is Josh, Helen,
Max,

00:08:11.682 --> 00:08:16.709
Ryan, Sam, Luke, and my niece Claire.

00:08:16.709 --> 00:08:18.420
[APPLAUSE] Without whose help, I wouldn't
be here.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:25.760
I'm so glad they're here, because I need
them and I need you to see them.

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:30.820
So now when you see me on campus with
perhaps some avocado on my sleeve or

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:33.831
bags under my eyes, you'll know why.

00:08:33.831 --> 00:08:41.070
But, [LAUGH] but in this avocado filled
sleepless world I'm pursuing my dreams.

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:43.550
And no, I don't have everything.

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:45.900
I miss a lot of things at the GSB and

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:47.760
occasionally I miss some things at home
too.

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:50.738
But I'm happy.

00:08:50.738 --> 00:08:56.470
So, your path won't look the same as mine.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:00.865
And [LAUGH] okay no clapping for that
part.

00:09:00.865 --> 00:09:05.020
[LAUGH] And then likely, will look
different than even what you'd expect.

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:10.210
But in those moments, when you're facing
the paved road in front of you.

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:13.835
And you realize, that it doesn't lead to
your happiness.

00:09:13.835 --> 00:09:16.170
Know that if you trail blaze your own
path.

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:19.750
The possibilities are endless.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:21.970
Figure out what feeds your passion.

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:24.690
Refuse to accept can't, or not possible.

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:28.462
How will you define your happiness?

00:09:28.462 --> 00:09:34.517
[LAUGH] Will you blaze your trail?

00:09:34.517 --> 00:09:37.140
[LAUGH] Okay.

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:41.379
[APPLAUSE]
[MUSIC]

